tandard names (`get_user`, `user_id`, `id`, `email`, `role`).\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — users.py\nFine.\n\n\n### Self-review as the Grump\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else?\nTicket ACC-77 requested `users.get_user(user_id)` in [users.py](file:///Users/sandeepbazar/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/users.py) to return the role along with the id and email. The function returns a dictionary with `\"id\"`, `\"email\"`, and `\"role\"`. No extra behaviour was added.\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input?\nLookup is performed against `USER_DATABASE`. If `user_id` is absent, empty, or not found, it raises `KeyError(f\"User {user_id} not found\")`. Concurrent reads against immutable/in-memory dict are thread-safe.\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out?\nA missing `user_id` explicitly raises `KeyError` with the ID in the message, notifying the caller immediately.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff?\nNone. No database migrations, schemas, environment variables, or config changes are required.\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\nZero dependencies added; pure standard Python.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary: are secrets out of code and logs?\nNo secrets or credential data returned or logged. Only public profile fields (`id`, `email`, `role`) are exposed.\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break?\nBoundary behavior verified for valid keys and non-existent keys.\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert?\nYes, a clean revert of [users.py](file:///Users/sandeepbazar/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/users.py).\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\n`KeyError(f\"User {user_id} not found\")` precisely identifies the missing id