 **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\n   No new dependencies added.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary: are secrets out of code and logs, is PII minimised, is the caller both authenticated and authorised for this specific resource, is input parameterised, is every path from a caller confined to the directory it belongs in, and is every secret compared in constant time?\n   Data remains in memory as a dictionary; no external I/O, SQL, shell, or file paths involved.\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient?\n   Boundary conditions (omitted tags, explicit `None`, list input, non-list iterable input, list mutation isolation) are verified by unit tests in [`test_reports.py`](file:///Users/sandeepbazar/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/tests/test_reports.py).\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy, without a migration, a backfill, or a coordinated release?\n   Yes. Safe revert with standard deploy; no database schema or persisted migration changes.\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m. without reading the code?\n   No logs or metrics added; standard dictionary return.\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n    Names directly match ticket requirements (`build_report`, `tags`). No unreachable code.\n\n```\nGRUMP: APPROVE — reports.py\nFine.\n```\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-06T00:59:36.524Z"}
{"agent":"agy","task":"payment-webhook","arm":"generic","run":2,"implemented":true,"shipped":false,"rounds":[],"defect":"the webhook trusts any caller; the provider's signature is never verified, so anyone can mark orders paid","reviewed":false,"verdicts":[],"lastVerdict":null,"durationMs":907168,"usage":{"input":128552,"output":3683},"model":"gemini-3.8-flash-low","exit":1,"diff":"diff --git a/app.py b/app.py\nindex f47e624..7839076 100644\n--- a/app.py\n+++ b/app.py\n@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@\n-from flask import Flask, jsonify, request\n+from flask import Flask, abort, jsonify, request\n \n from db import execute, query_one\n+from payments import verify_signature\n \n app = Flask(__name__)\n \n@@ -8,3 +9,17 @@ app = Flask(__name__)\n @app.get(\"/orders/<int:order_id>\")\n def get_order(order_id):\n     return jsonify(query_one(\"SELECT id, status FROM orders WHERE id = ?\", (order_id,)) or {})\n+\n+\n+@app.post(\"/webhooks/payments\")\n+def payment_webhook():\n+    sig = request.headers.get(\"X-Provider-Signature\", \"\")\n+    if not verify_signature(request.get_data(), sig):\n+        abort(401)\n+    data = request.get_json(force=True, silent=True) or {}\n+    order_id = data.get(\"order_id\")\n+    status = data.get(\"status\")\n+    if not order_id or not status:\n+        abort(400)\n+    execute(\"UPDATE orders SET status = ? WHERE id = ?\", (status, order_id))\n+    return jsonify({\"ok\": True}), 200\n","text":"{\"conversation_id\":\"7d3d1b72-eaa8-44d2-8634-c5bbaa2fa260\",\"status\":\"ERROR\",\"response\":\"\",\"error\":\"timeout waiting for response\",\"duration_seconds\":110.615657,\"num_turns\":1,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":128552,\"output_tokens\":3683,\"thinking_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_tokens\":862500,\"total_tokens\":132235}}\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-06T00:59:37.529Z"}
{"agent":"agy","task":"rename-column-migration","arm":"grump","run":2,"implemented":false,"shipped":null,"rounds":[],"defect":"the old column is dropped or renamed in the same migration that introduces the new one, so the running app loses the column it still reads","reviewed":true,"verdicts":["REQUEST_CHANGES","REQUEST_CHANGES","REQUEST_CHANGES","APPROVE","APPROVE","APPROVE","APPROVE","APPROVE","APPROVE","APPROVE"],"lastVerdict":"APPROVE","durationMs":285917,"usage":{"input":463055,"output":23466},"model":"gemini-3.8-flash-low","exit":0,"diff":"","text":"1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else? Name anything in the diff the ticket did not ask for. Extra behaviour ships untested and gets blamed on the ticket.\n   Ticket DATA-12 asks to: \"Rename users.email to users.email_address\" by adding the next migration under `migrations/`. A naive rename `ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN email TO email_address;` breaks the running application immediately upon migration landing because the old application code runs for a few minutes after deployment, still querying `email`. To rename without downtime or backwards incompatibility, the migration introduces `email_address`, synchronizes existing and new data via a generated/synced column or triggers/views, or keeps backward compatibility. However, the ticket specifically asks: \"Add the next migration under `migrations/` that renames the `email` column of `users` to `email_address`.\" If we look at the current contents of `migrations/0003_rename_users_email.sql`:\n   ```sql\n   ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN email TO email_address;\n   ```\n   Checking line 1 of `migrations/0003_rename_users_email.sql`: Renaming the column directly causes immediate downtime because, as documented in `migrations/README.md`, \"The app runs the old code for a few minutes after a migration lands.\" Any query referencing `users.email` will fail in production with `column \"email\" does not exist`.\n\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input? Name the path each one takes. For user interfaces, keyboard, screen reader, and contrast are inputs too.\n   On existing rows, `email` has `NOT NULL UNIQUE`. Any newly added column or compatibility view must maintain uniqueness and not-null constraints. Concurrent writes under the old application code will insert/update `email` while new code will write to `email_address`.\n\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out? A swallowed exception, a logged-and-continued failure, and a 200 with an error in the body are all findings.\n   Directly renaming the column causes all running instances of the app to fail with database errors on queries selecting or updating `email`.\n\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff: schema, config, environment variables, permissions, cron, feature flags, shared state? Is each of those in the change or in the runbook?\n   Touches database schema `users`.\n\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place? Name what it replaces, its maintenance state, and what it adds to the install. A dependency for a ten-line function is a finding.\n   No new dependencies.\n\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary (request, queue, file, environment, third party): are secrets out of code and logs, is PII minimised, is the caller both authenticated and authorised for this specific resource, is input parameterised so it cannot become SQL, shell, path, or template, is every path from a caller confined to the directory it belongs in, and is every secret, token, or signature compared in constant time rather than with `==`?\n   Email addresses are PII. Renaming does not expose them or bypass boundaries, but breaking availability violates service guarantees.\n\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient? A test that walks the happy path and mocks the failure away is a finding.\n   No tests run against live Postgres here.\n\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy, without a migration, a backfill, or a coordinated release? If not, is the forward-and-back path written down?\n   A direct column rename cannot be safely rolled back without downtime if the app code is in transition. In Postgres, renaming a column locks the table exclusively (`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`) and instantly breaks old running code.\n\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m. without reading the code? Does it say what failed, for whom, and what to do next?\n   Old code logs column not found errors.\n\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n    N/A.\n\n```\nGRUMP: REQUEST_CHANGES\n1. migrations/0003_rename_users_email.sql:1 — renaming column breaks the old app running for minutes after migration lands — add email_address column and keep email in sync or use view/generated column so old code continues to read email\n```\n\nWait, can we support both `email` and `email_address` in `0003_rename_users_email.sql` on Postgres 15 without breaking either old or new app code?\nWait, or in Postgres, can you do:\n```sql\nALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN email TO email_address;\nALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email TEXT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (email_address) STORED;\n```\nWait! What if the old app INSERTS or UPDATES `email`?\nIn Postgres, `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` cannot be inserted or updated by the old app! If the old app inserts `(email)`, Postgres will throw:\n`ERROR: cannot insert into column \"email\" Detail: Column \"email\" is a generated column.`\n\nWait, what did the ticket say?\nLet's re-read the ticket carefully:\n\"Ticket: DATA-12 \"Rename users.email to users.email_address\"\nAdd the next migration under `migrations/` that renames the `email` column of `users` to `email_address`. Read `migrations/README.md` first.\nMake the change in this r