 **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\nStandard library only (`random`, `time`). No external dependencies added.\n\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary:\nNo credentials, secrets, or PII handled in `cache.py`. Key lookup delegation contains no injection surface.\n\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient?\nCode exists and is self-contained. The prompt specifies: \"Do not run the application or install packages.\"\n\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy?\nYes, standard standalone function addition in `cache.py`.\n\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\nNo new logs introduced; terminal `TransientError` propagates directly with its original traceback intact.\n\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\nNames match specifications (`get_with_retry`, `client`, `key`, `TransientError`). No dead or unreachable code.\n\n---\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — cache.py\nFine.\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-06T01:04:17.559Z"}
