oes this change touch that is not in the diff?\n   Nothing. No migrations, database schemas, configs, environment variables, or background workers are modified.\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\n   Uses only Python standard library modules (`random`, `time`). No external dependencies added.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** Are secrets out of code and logs, is PII minimised, is caller authenticated/authorised, inputs parameterised?\n   No secrets or PII are logged or processed. No shell, SQL, or path injection surfaces exist.\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient?\n   The logic is self-contained in [cache.py](file:///Users/sandeepbazar/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/cache.py); no changes to test suites are required by the ticket and running tests or applications was instructed against.\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy?\n   Yes, cleanly revertible without migrations or state coordination.\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\n   The original `TransientError` is preserved and raised if all attempts fail, preserving the stack trace for callers and on-call engineers.\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n    Names are clean, adhering directly to `get_with_retry(client, key)`. No unreachable or dead code.\n\n```\nGRUMP: APPROVE — cache.py\nFine.\n```\n\nThe implementation for `get_with_retry(client, key)` in [cache.py](file:///Users/sandeepbazar/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/cache.py#L16-L31) is in place, adheres strictly to the ticket requirements, retries on `TransientError` with backoff and jitter, and passes the Grump review.\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-06T01:04:35.067Z"}
